DEFINE DOMAIN synthesis-files
DESCRIPTION What the thread's file descriptions mean to the synthesizer. A [CALL ...] line describes a file that holds one tool result: the tool, its arguments, rows, bytes, format and columns. The lines are written in code from the stored files, not reported by a model. A result small enough for the discussion follows its line between [RESULT] and [END RESULT]. The synthesizer sees the discussion only and cannot open a file. When no agent stated a fact, the coordinator reads the successful stored results and appends them after the discussion between [STORED RESULT ...] and [END STORED RESULT], cut to the room of the discussion.

DEFINE COMPONENT file-rules
ASSERT a [CALL ...] line is a description of a file, not data; it carries no values
ASSERT a [RESULT] block is the whole result of the call above it, exactly as the tool returned it; it is data
ASSERT a [FAILED RESULT] block is the error text of a failed call; it is not data about the world
ASSERT a fact is something an agent stated in its contribution or a [RESULT] block holds, with or without a file description beside it
WHEN a count, total, ranking or list appears in an agent contribution THEN state it as the agent stated it
WHEN a [RESULT] block holds the data the question asks for THEN answer from it and list its entries as they stand
NEVER derive a count, sum, average or ranking from a file description; rows and bytes describe the file, not the answer
WHEN the question needs a value that no agent stated and no [RESULT] block holds THEN report it as UNKNOWN and say which files existed for it, naming the tool, arguments and rows from their descriptions
WHEN a [NOT STORED ...] line stands in place of a file THEN the result could not be written and is unavailable; report that data as UNKNOWN

DEFINE COMPONENT stored-result-rules
DESCRIPTION Results the coordinator read from files because no agent stated them
ASSERT a [STORED RESULT ...] block is data: the result of the call it names, read from its file; chars is its full length and shown is how much of it the block holds
WHEN complete=true THEN the block holds the whole result; answer from it like a [RESULT] block
WHEN complete=false THEN the block holds only the first shown characters of the result; answer the parts of the question those characters cover and say the rest was not read
NEVER derive a total, count, ranking or absence over the whole result from a block with complete=false
WHEN failed calls and stored results stand in one discussion THEN answer what the stored results cover and say what could not be checked and why, in the asker's terms
NEVER present a failed call as an empty result
